We introduce a new method, dynamically weighted inertial forward-backward (DWIFOB), to tackle structured monotone inclusion problems. By incorporating the forward-backward algorithm and the extrapolation technique from Anderson acceleration, we develop a method that converges at the rate of $O(k^{-2})$. Furthermore, we provide implementations of DWIFOB for specific monotone inclusion problems such as the proximal maximal monotone operator and the split monotone variational inequality problems. Additionally, under certain conditions, especially when the proximal maximal monotone operator is involved, we enhance the $O(k^{-2})$ convergence rate to $O(k^{-3})$. Our experiments, using sample problems, demonstrate that DWIFOB achieves a faster convergence rate compared to existing methods. Moreover, we observe that even in the absence of any form of acceleration, DWIFOB still exhibits improved performance and resilience. Our research offers an alternative solution to tackle structured monotone inclusion problems, especially when dealing with slow convergence or oscillatory behavior. The code for DWIFOB is available on GitHub. Ultimately, we hypothesize that DWIFOB may serve as a robust and efficient problem-solving approach for a broad range of applications. Introduction and background: We aim to address the solution of a general monotone inclusion problem, defined as finding $x\in X$, such that $0\in T(x)$. Here, $X$ denotes a given vector space, and $T:\mathbb{R}^{n}\to\mathbb{R}^{n}$ represents the set-valued monotone operator function, satisfying $\forall x,y\in\mathbb{R}^{n},\forall h\in\mathbb{R}^{n}$, $T(x)+T(y)\supseteq T(x)+T(y)+\mathbb{R}^{n}$. Here, $\mathbb{R}^{n}$ represents the set of all vectors from $\mathbb{R}^{n}$. The forward-backward algorithm, introduced by Martinet in 1959, is a primal-dual method designed to solve monotone inclusion problems, defined by $0\in T(x)$. It can be represented by the iterative formula: \begin{equation}
